      "annotations" : [ {
        "message" : "The term \"Third Party\" as defined in Article 4(10) GDPR refers to any natural or legal person, public authority, agency, or body other than the data subject, the controller, the processor, and persons who, under the direct authority of the controller or processor, are authorized to process personal data. In practical terms for software architecture, this means that any entity outside the core data processing roles—such as external service providers, partners, or other external systems receiving personal data—qualifies as a third party. Recognizing third parties is crucial when designing data flows and access controls, as data transfers or disclosures to third parties trigger specific GDPR obligations, including ensuring lawful processing, data protection safeguards, and transparency towards data subjects. It is important to distinguish third parties from processors or authorized personnel within the controller’s organization, as the latter operate under direct authority and are not considered third parties under the GDPR.",
